  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q77",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "77",
    "prompt": "Show that $\\dfrac{x^2}{x-1}=x+1+\\dfrac{1}{x-1}$, and hence differentiate $y=\\dfrac{x^2}{x-1}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$y'=1-\\dfrac{1}{(x-1)^2}$, $x\\ne1$.\n\nCombine terms to establish the identity, then differentiate its right-hand side.",
      "worked": "For $x\\ne1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x^2}{x-1}&=x+1+\\frac{1}{x-1}\\\\\\frac{x^2}{x-1}&=\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)+1}{x-1}\\\\\\frac{x^2}{x-1}&=\\frac{x^2}{x-1}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $y=x+1+(x-1)^{-1}$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}y'&=1+0-(x-1)^{-2}(1)\\\\y'&=1-\\frac{1}{(x-1)^2}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 64
  },
